speechtext:
Very well. Even if "the .whole of it were authorized by statute, that would not be a satisfactory explanation. The expenditure of this Parliament is authorized by statute, and yet my right hon. friend knows how that expenditure is criticised, and he knows that parties in Opposition have found that expenditures, although authorized by statute, are sometimes very badly handled. That is the objection I raise. Even if the expenditure be authorized by statute, the man who has authority to spend the money ought to be in this House or have a representative here; or if you like it better, the minister who is responsible to this House ought to have a representative under his control, and this expenditure ought to be made also under his control. The other explanation given by the right -hon. gentleman is rather a surprise to me, namely, that the expenditure is under the immediate control of the Auditor General. The other night we had a discussion in this House, and I addressed the following remarks to the acting Minister of Finance, or the Minister of Finance, I do not know which, as that matter is a little hazy just now: I note that in your estimates you are decreasing the amount which goes- to pay the employees of your department. He admitted that. I asked him: How can you do it whep your work of- auditing is increasing on account of the larger expenditures to which the country is being put as a result of the war? I did not have time to look at Hansard, but what I gathered from his answer was this;
The details of the war expenditure do-not go to the Auditor General to be examined and passed upon. Hie answer seemed to- 'be very reasonable, in view of the objection that, I was -making, that the staff -was decreasing as the work was increasing. It seemed very reasonable to suppose that somebody else -must have been assuming a part- of the burden. My right hon. friend says that the Auditor General has been -there and has examined the accounts. It may be all very well to- say that the Auditor General is assuming responsibility, but I contend that the Auditor -General is our officer, he is our servant, he is independent of the Government, and for a very good reason. He is the watch dog of the treasury, as -we are pleased to call him, but I do- not think it is fair that he should delegate bis powers to any man over -whom he has- not immediate supervision. He may find a -good- man, but I

da not think he should have an assistant auditor or a staff of. auditors across the seas to perform work which ought to toe performed under his immediate -supervision in his office, and for which work he should be open to criticism. It may toe that the darkness is viery thick, as analyzed toy my hon. friend, tout that does not account for it, and that does not make me feel satisfied with the audit of the expenditure made overseas toy the Department of Militia and Defence.

In the case of a tubercular man, our arrangement was that when he came from overseas he left England as a soldier, he arrived in Canada as a soldier, still on the strength of the Canadian Expeditionary Force. The intention of the medical service was that that man should be placed under immediate observation in one of the homes, and if it were found that he was suffering from acute tuberculosis, he would immediately have his pay adjusted up to date, would go on full pension, and then go into one of the sanatoria which still remain under the control of the Invalided Soldiers' Commission, which was formerly the Military Hospitals Commission. They are retaining under their control the sanatoria and vocational training camp which will
10	p.m. take charge of that man after he has been discharged from the service. He is off our strength, and is given his military pay up to date, and then goes off on full pension. The same might be said of the insane cases. There "are, unfortunately, many cases of insane men coming back. Arrangements have been made with the other department, and the old Military Hospitals Commission, through some of the various provinces whereby they will take care of these patients. If that man has a wife or children his pension is paid to them. If he has no wife or child, and he is entitled to a pen-39}
sion, that money is held, and some arrangement is made with the local government about caring for that man while he is in the hospital. There are many cases of epileptic, and possibly nervous cases, which have not reached that stage that will be discharged and go back to their own care, but if they require further medical treatment, this new medical department will look after them, but they are chiefly charged with the vocational training end of it.
